Discovery global/per-organization lease ownership
=================================================

Discovery requires a confirmed SET NX EX acquisition before its registered
Celery task wrapper runs the controller discovery coroutine. A unique token
belongs to the lexical invocation. Existing contention returns the existing
success/skipped/already_running result. Store unavailability or ambiguous
acquisition acknowledgement raises a fixed safe error before discovery runs.
An accepted but unacknowledged SET can leave its key until expiry; the worker
must not assume ownership or automatically repeat external effects.

Cleanup atomically deletes a key only if its stored token still matches this
invocation. A late or repeated cleanup cannot remove a successor or another
organization's acquisition. Release transport failures are logged using a fixed
message and do not mask completed effects or the original body exception.
Sentinel-aware clients use bounded command/connect timeouts and are closed on
success and failure. Closing an already-used client cannot change the outcome.

The existing global and per-organization key names and 900-second expiry remain
unchanged. Different scopes retain their existing independent lease behavior.
Per-controller locks are a separate layer. This change does not add lease
renewal, durable submission, exactly-once execution or hardware-operation
fencing: a paused worker may resume after expiry while a successor is running.
Stop old workers before rollout. Old code uses unconditional DEL and can remove
new leases during a mixed-version deployment; new code cannot prevent that.
Raw Celery infrastructure storage/logging and successful task payload contracts
remain separate privacy boundaries. Acquisition errors from this helper expose
only a fixed category; ordinary discovery-body errors are not sanitized here.

Regression tests cover unavailable/ambiguous acquisition, stale ownership,
contention, cleanup failure, scoped wrapper execution and preserved exceptions.
Separate-process transport probes use real Redis or the qualified Valkey image
with a synthetic discovery coroutine: no hardware acceptance, broker-delivery
qualification or failover/partition safety guarantee is inferred from them.
